'''Conway's Game of Life''' is a two-dimensional cellular automaton devised by John Horton Conway and publicized by Martin Gardner in 1970. A grid of cells repeatedly changes between live and dead states according to fixed local rules.

== Formal rule ==
Let x_t(i,j)∈{0,1} and let n_t(i,j) be the sum of the eight surrounding cell states. The synchronous update is x_(t+1)=1 when n_t=3, or when x_t=1 and n_t=2; otherwise it is zero. The standard rule is B3/S23 on the infinite square lattice. A finite simulation must specify dead, periodic or other boundary conditions, which can alter outcomes.

<pre>
for each cell (i,j) in current grid:
  n = sum of 8 neighbor values from current grid
  next[i,j] = (n == 3) or (current[i,j] and n == 2)
current = next
</pre>

== Patterns and reproducibility ==
A 2×2 live block in an otherwise empty grid is a still life. Three adjacent horizontal live cells form a blinker: one generation later they are vertical, and after another generation horizontal. A glider repeats its shape after four generations displaced diagonally. Test these patterns away from boundaries to verify simultaneous updates. Sparse implementations can evaluate only live cells and their adjacent candidates, provided all potentially born cells are included.

== Computation and limits ==
Life supports engineered logic and universal computation under idealized constructions; this is an existential result about suitable initial configurations, not a guarantee that arbitrary patterns compute usefully. Finite grids with fixed finite state necessarily eventually repeat. Long-lived complexity illustrates emergence from simple rules without demonstrating biological life, purposeful agency or consciousness.

